<!-- SOC-AI - rule 100770 (level 12). Domain: threat_hunting,linux,post_exploitation,
     Naming convention and load-order trap: see rules/README.md -->
<group name="threat_hunting,linux,post_exploitation,">

  <!-- T1136 / T1053 / T1548.003 / T1543.002 / T1556.003 - a WRITE to a
       persistence path, seen through the auditd `-k audit-wazuh-persist` watches
       (/etc/passwd, /etc/group, /etc/sudoers*, /etc/cron*, /var/spool/cron,
       /etc/systemd/system, /etc/pam.d).

       WHY THIS RULE EXISTS ON TOP OF THE FIM (100740-100749): measured live,
       real-time FIM proved INTERMITTENT on /etc/passwd - three consecutive
       modifications, a single alert. Resting the detection of a UID 0 backdoor on
       that one channel was therefore a gamble. The auditd watch is deterministic
       (one kernel event per syscall) and additionally brings what the FIM never
       provides: WHO wrote (exe, uid, auid). The FIM keeps the complementary
       advantage of CONTENT (the diff), hence keeping both.

       Level 12 rather than 13: the rule covers a broad perimeter, including
       legitimate writes (a package creating a service account, dpkg dropping a
       cron file). The high-impact cases stay carried by the specific rules:
       100744/100762 (UID 0), 100745 (sudoers), 100748 (preload). -->

  <rule id="100770" level="12">
    <if_group>audit</if_group>
    <field name="audit.key" type="pcre2">^audit-wazuh-persist$</field>
    <description>Persistence: write to $(audit.file.name) by $(audit.exe) (uid=$(audit.uid))</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1136</id>
      <id>T1053.003</id>
      <id>T1543.002</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

</group>
